 "genre_note": "Cluster c13 is science journalism from seven outlets, and genre shaped the decomposition more than subject matter did. Three document shapes dominate. (1) Single-study reports (cannabis/pregnancy, Mediterranean lifestyle, zinc-iodine batteries, cavefish/keto, jellyfish learning, hydrogel water harvesting) follow an almost invariant arc: finding, method, mechanism, limitations. That arc splits cleanly into empirical_observation + procedure + model + counter_argument, and the counter_argument is real, not manufactured \u2014 the limitations paragraph in these pieces routinely reverses the headline (doc_0604's stillbirth link vanishes under adjustment; doc_0466's napping result is undercut by the authors' own missing data on nap frequency and duration). Dropping those paragraphs as routine boilerplate would have deleted the scope condition on the central claim, so they were assigned in every case. (2) Two documents are listicles or photo roundups (doc_0325, ten unrelated solar discoveries; doc_0556, a gallery of aurora eyewitness accounts). Size alone would have merged each into one note; topical coherence forced ten and seven notes respectively. doc_0325 in particular is one 1,589-word document containing ten subjects that share only the word \"sun\". (3) Two are long-form: an Eos technical review (doc_0139) and an NBC profile (doc_0233), each decomposing into a dozen or more notes because they genuinely develop many distinct claims.\n\nBuilding-block distribution: empirical_observation dominates (about 80 of 155 notes), then concept (about 35) and model (about 25). argument and counter_argument appear about 20 times combined, almost always as a named scientist's caveat or an outside expert's dissent \u2014 Brembs on jellyfish sample size, Sotos-Prieto on reverse causation in napping, the pattern-effect magnitude dispute in doc_0139. hypothesis is genuinely rare, as expected of news: only two notes qualify, both from doc_0325 (superflares as the spark for life; solar neutrinos as a dark-matter probe), and both only because they state an untested prediction with an explicit \"not yet detected\" clause. procedure appears where a method or policy has ordered steps (MXene synthesis, urine screening, Bawendi's synthesis, antibiotic stewardship, the MHRA/NICE approval route). No navigation notes were produced.\n\nThree entity notes are shared across documents rather than duplicated: global_hiv_burden_2022 and antiretroviral_therapy_and_viral_suppression draw on both doc_0238 and doc_0472, and crispr_cas_system_mechanism draws on doc_0472 (HIV gene therapy) and doc_0566 (antibacterial CRISPR) \u2014 the two documents describe the same machinery for opposite purposes, and one note carrying both is what lets a single retrieval span them.\n\nCoverage: every document clears 88.9% and 19 of 20 clear 90%. The two lowest are structural, not editorial. doc_0139 (92.4%) is an Eos review whose last third is a reference list, acknowledgments, author affiliations and copyright; the twelve bibliography entries were assigned to the notes whose claims they actually ground (Andrews 2022 to the disputed-magnitude note, Wills 2022 to the model-bias note, and so on) rather than dropped wholesale, since a citation attached to its claim is evidence, not chrome. doc_0084 (88.9%, the only document under the 90% aim) is a Spanish translation of an Eos piece carrying an unusually heavy boilerplate load \u2014 translation notice, translator credit, ENGAGE educator promotion, byline and two copyright lines, together 142 of its 1,283 words; nothing substantive was dropped there. Elsewhere the drops are article titles, section headers, \"Related:\" link stubs, the Science News Explores newsletter sign-up form (recurring verbatim in doc_0247, doc_0261, doc_0285 and doc_0436), image-credit lines and \"Image 1 of 3\" gallery scaffolding. Two apparent chrome blocks were deliberately assigned instead: doc_0391's correction notice, which carries the load-bearing fact that Ryan Lee works at Shriners Hospital for Children Honolulu and not the University of Hawai'i Manoa as the body text implies, and the pull-quotes in doc_0139 and doc_0084, which duplicate body sentences and were routed to the same note as the sentence they quote rather than left unassigned.",
